  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100085",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-81355",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a28c4320-e2dd-5195-81ac-b910c2328691",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type I Region",
      "problem": "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2-{x}^{2}}x\\,dy\\,dx.$",
      "worked_solution": "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2-{x}^{2}}x\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2-{x}^{2}}dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[y]_{{x}^{2}}^{2-{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(2-{x}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})\\,dx & =2{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
