  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100385",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-132410",
      "knowledge_point_id": "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51",
      "knowledge_point": "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given",
      "problem": "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the lines $y=2x+4$ and $y=4-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=4.$ What is ${∬}_{D}x{e}^{y}\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=2x+4$ and $y=4-x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x+4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}y-2 \\\\ y=4-x & \\,⟹\\,x=4-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100385/assets/da7778322523d0d18a5d5e24af075709958d329fc0053c836fd6637aa912752d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\frac{1}{2}y-2\\le x\\le 4-y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{e}^{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2-2}^{4-y}x{e}^{y}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{\\int }_{y/2-2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y}x{e}^{y}\\,dx\\,dy",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2-2}^{4-y}x{e}^{y}\\,dx\\,dy",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{\\int }_{0}^{4-y}{\\int }_{y/2-2}^{4}x{e}^{y}\\,dx\\,dy",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{\\int }_{y/2-2}^{4-y}{\\int }_{0}^{4}x{e}^{y}\\,dx\\,dy",
              "option": "3",
              "source_option": "d"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{4-y}^{y/2-2}x{e}^{y}\\,dx\\,dy",
              "option": "4",
              "source_option": "e"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source_answer_policy": {
        "version": "revealed-ma-answer-v1",
        "feedback_sha256": "999d898e624b745f86bd7155fe31352fa114290565b68a4958f41488e6561c2f"
      },
      "difficulty": "moderate",
      "provenance": {
        "activity_question": "question-132410",
        "kp_title": "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given",
        "kp_href": null
      },
      "topic_id": 2152,
      "source_file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100385/state.json",
