  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100385",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-89550",
      "knowledge_point_id": "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100385/assets/d6f00e63a577dff29021e50add69cf916b3f74ed90530b5f4c9f46295950b416.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}{x}^{3}\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ above the $x$-axis, as shown in the picture.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}} & =\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} \\\\ 1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =4-{y}^{2} \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =16-4{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{y}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100385/assets/ec8853d64b58c40935b769745d5adb9ebc5c84b7f9407cc93dff4b809ed64ad5.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{3}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/4}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,{x}^{3}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/4}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,{x}^{3}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/4}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,{x}^{3}\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,{[\\,\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}\\,]}_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/4}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{4}[{(4-{y}^{2})}^{2}-{(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4})}^{2}]dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,[(16-8{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})-(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{16})]dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,(15-\\frac{15}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{15}{16}{y}^{4})dy \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{16})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{4}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{16})dy & =\\frac{15}{4}{[y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{80}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}[(2-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{2}{5})-0] \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{16}{15}",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{15}{4}",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
